{"name":"Recycled Glass","hex":"#b7c3b7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"recycled","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of recycle"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That has been through a recycling process."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/recycled"},{"term":"glass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An amorphous solid, often transparent substance, usually made by melting silica sand with various additives (for most purposes, a mixture of soda, potash and lime is added).","Any amorphous solid (one without a regular crystal lattice)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fit with glass; to glaze.","To enclose in glass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glass recycling","blocks":[{"text":"Glass recycling is the process of collecting, processing, and re-manufacturing waste glass into new products. Glass is ideal for recycling because it is not degraded by normal use. Scrap glass material, known as cullet, is used in glass manufacturing to reduce the consumption of energy and new raw materials. There are two types of cullet:"},{"items":["Internal cullet comprises waste glass generated by glass production processes themselves, including quality control rejects, material from production transitions, and manufacturing offcuts.","External cullet consists of post-industrial and post-consumer waste glass collected through organised recycling programmes."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Glas_aus_Aufbereitungsanlage_bunt_-_glass_cullet_various_%28Alter_Fritz%29.JPG/330px-Glas_aus_Aufbereitungsanlage_bunt_-_glass_cullet_various_%28Alter_Fritz%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass_recycling"},{"title":"Recycling","blocks":[{"text":"Recycling is the process of converting waste materials into new materials and objects. This concept often includes the recovery of energy from waste materials. The recyclability of a material depends on its ability to reacquire the properties it had in its original state. It is an alternative to \"conventional\" waste disposal that can save material and help lower greenhouse gas emissions. It can also prevent the waste of potentially useful materials and reduce the consumption of fresh raw materials, reducing energy use, air pollution and water pollution."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/RecyclingSymbolGreen.png/330px-RecyclingSymbolGreen.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recycling"},{"title":"Glass","blocks":[{"text":"Glass is an amorphous (non-crystalline) solid. Because it is often transparent and chemically inert, glass has found widespread practical, technological, and decorative use in window panes, tableware, and optics. Some common objects made of glass are named after the material, e.g., a \"glass\" for drinking, \"glasses\" for vision correction, and a \"magnifying glass\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg/330px-Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Beer_bottles_2018_G1.jpg/500px-Beer_bottles_2018_G1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Beer_bottles_2018_G1.jpg/1280px-Beer_bottles_2018_G1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Beer_bottles_2018_G1.jpg/500px-Beer_bottles_2018_G1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bottles in different colours","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeer_bottles_2018_G1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Glass_salvage_containers_in_front_of_Park_Tenreuken_during_the_day_%28Auderghem%2C_Belgium%2C_DSCF2709%29.jpg/500px-Glass_salvage_containers_in_front_of_Park_Tenreuken_during_the_day_%28Auderghem%2C_Belgium%2C_DSCF2709%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Glass_salvage_containers_in_front_of_Park_Tenreuken_during_the_day_%28Auderghem%2C_Belgium%2C_DSCF2709%29.jpg/1280px-Glass_salvage_containers_in_front_of_Park_Tenreuken_during_the_day_%28Auderghem%2C_Belgium%2C_DSCF2709%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Glass_salvage_containers_in_front_of_Park_Tenreuken_during_the_day_%28Auderghem%2C_Belgium%2C_DSCF2709%29.jpg/500px-Glass_salvage_containers_in_front_of_Park_Tenreuken_during_the_day_%28Auderghem%2C_Belgium%2C_DSCF2709%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Public glass waste collection point in Belgium. The different receptacles are for different colours of glass.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGlass_salvage_containers_in_front_of_Park_Tenreuken_during_the_day_(Auderghem%2C_Belgium%2C_DSCF2709).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Altglasleerung.JPG/500px-Altglasleerung.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Altglasleerung.JPG/1280px-Altglasleerung.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Altglasleerung.JPG/500px-Altglasleerung.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_camp